================================================================================
FINAL SUMMARY  (slide_*.py, exact rational arithmetic throughout)
================================================================================
Q1  MAIN HYPOTHESIS, as stated ("under slide A with small enough e>0 a point of
    degPts is lost IFF it is supported ONLY by P-side v-1"):

      CONFIRMED whenever P[v-1],P[v],P[v+1] are NOT collinear   3222/3222
      REFUTED   when they ARE collinear                            0/25
                (targeted family slide_advers.py --kind colP)

    REFINED STATEMENT, 3515/3515 exact set equality:
      lost = {}                                     if orientR(P[v-1],P[v],P[v+1]) = 0
      lost = {X in degPts : suppP(X) = {v-1}}       otherwise
    Reason for the exception: SimpleClosed forces a collinear apex to lie
    STRICTLY BETWEEN its neighbours, so E = P[v]-P[v+1] is ANTI-parallel to
    side v-1; side v-1 SHRINKS along its own line and side v grows by exactly
    the same sub-segment, leaving the P-boundary UNCHANGED AS A POINT SET.

    Sub-lemmas, 1600/1600 each:
      L1  |crossPairs| never decreases under a small-e slide A.
      L2  every lost degenerate point is a Q-vertex and never a P-vertex.
      L3  the only point ever GAINED is the moved apex itself (it is gained
          exactly when the open ray P[v+1]->P[v] enters a Q-side at P[v]).

Q2  old apex still a degenerate point after slide A: 3515/3515 at e<=1e-8 and
    at EVERY admissible e of the 11-rung ladder.  This is a THEOREM, not an
    observation: new side v = [P[v]+eE, P[v+1]] with E = P[v]-P[v+1] equals
    [P[v+1] + (1+e)(P[v]-P[v+1]), P[v+1]] and contains P[v] for every e>0;
    P[v] = Q[j] is a Q-vertex, so it stays in degPts.  Same for slide B.

Q3  M_pt CAN STRICTLY DECREASE under an admissible small-e slide A.
    Joint (|degPts lost|, dCross) over admissible small-e slide A, mix corpus:
    the cell (1,0) is populated -- 40 of 356 admissible instances in the
    slide_final corpus.  "Nothing is lost" is NOT the generic truth in the
    adversarial family, and a crossing-gain lemma is NOT available: dCross = 0
    while one degenerate point disappears.

Q4  "slide A or slide B always works" is FALSE.  The failures are (Fin)
    failures and are e-INDEPENDENT (slide A keeps the new side v on the OLD
    side v's LINE), so no shrinking of e rescues them -- verified on a ladder
    down to 1e-40 in slide_deep.py.

Q5  the adversarial case (a degenerate point on side v-1 AND another on side v,
    both besides the coincidence) is ABUNDANT, not rare: 200 instances built
    from 288 random P draws.  The attack is STRONG.
================================================================================
